course: biology
lesson: 289
title: Food from around the world: how crops travelled the globe
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A pizza is a world tour on a plate: the wheat in the base came from the Middle East, the tomatoes from the Americas, the olive oil from the Mediterranean and the pepper from India.
explain: Before 1492 Italy had no tomatoes, Ireland had no potatoes and nobody in Europe had tasted chocolate or chilli. After Columbus crossed the Atlantic, plants and animals sailed both ways, and this swap is called the Columbian exchange.
explain: It was not all good news: with the ships came diseases that killed huge numbers of the people already living in the Americas.
explain: Food tells history. Spices were once so precious that wars were fought over tiny islands, a boy's clever trick made vanilla cheap, and honey from ancient tombs could still be eaten today.
fact: Potatoes, tomatoes, maize, chocolate and chilli came from the Americas; wheat came from the Middle East, rice from Asia and coffee from Ethiopia.
fact: After Columbus, horses, cows and wheat went to the Americas, and potatoes and maize went to Europe and Africa; diseases crossed too and killed many people in the Americas.
fact: Oranges came from Asia and spread along trade routes; in English the colour orange was named after the fruit.
fact: Vanilla comes from the pods of an orchid; outside Mexico its flowers must be pollinated by hand, and in 1841 a 12-year-old enslaved boy on Réunion island, Edmond Albius, found the quick way to do it.
fact: In the Andes people made chuño, potatoes freeze-dried by night frost and day sun, which are light to carry and can last for years; Peru has thousands of kinds of potato.
fact: Sushi uses rice with vinegar and fish or vegetables; raw fish is safe only when fresh and handled carefully, sometimes frozen first to kill parasites; the oldest sushi was fish kept in fermenting rice.
fact: Olives are bitter on the tree and must be soaked or cured before eating, and some olive trees are over a thousand years old.
fact: The kiwi fruit came from China, where it was called the Chinese gooseberry; New Zealand farmers renamed it after their bird, and it has more vitamin C than an orange.
fact: Pepper, cinnamon, nutmeg and cloves came from Asia and far islands; sailors risked their lives for them, and wars were fought over tiny nutmeg islands.
fact: Honey can last thousands of years because it has very little water and is a bit acidic, so germs cannot grow; it should not be given to babies under one year.
fact: Food is cultural: frog legs in France, snails, horse meat in some countries and guinea pig in Peru, and other people may find our food odd too.
fact: Farmers breed giant pumpkins of over 1,000 kilograms, and the record is over 1,200 kilograms.
exercise: Which of these foods first came from the Americas? | Potatoes, tomatoes and maize | Wheat, rice and coffee beans | Olives, oranges and black pepper | Cinnamon, nutmeg and cloves
exercise: What was the Columbian exchange? | Plants, animals and diseases crossing the Atlantic both ways after Columbus | A big market in Spain where Columbus sold the spices he brought back from India | A swap of ships between Spain and Italy | A trade only in gold coins
exercise: What sad thing also crossed the ocean after 1492? | Diseases that killed many people in the Americas | Floods that drowned farms | Locusts that ate all the wheat in Europe for many years | Snow that killed crops
exercise: Where did oranges first come from? | Asia | Brazil | Ireland | Antarctica
exercise: Which plant does vanilla come from? | An orchid | A cactus | An oak tree | A grass
exercise: Who found the quick way to pollinate vanilla by hand? | Edmond Albius, a 12-year-old enslaved boy | Christopher Columbus, on his very first voyage | A French king | A Mexican bee keeper
exercise: What is chuño? | Potatoes freeze-dried by frost and sun in the Andes | A spicy Mexican sauce made from chillies and chocolate | A Japanese rice dish | A kind of olive
exercise: Why is raw fish for sushi sometimes frozen first? | Freezing kills parasites | It makes the fish taste sweeter | It turns the fish pink | It makes the rice stick
exercise: Why can you not eat an olive straight off the tree? | It is very bitter until soaked or cured | It is poisonous for ever, even when cooked | It is too hard to bite | It has no flesh
exercise: Where did the kiwi fruit come from? | China | New Zealand | Brazil | Spain
exercise: Why were spices once worth so much? | They came from very far away and the voyages were dangerous | They grew only in Europe | They were made of gold dust | Kings banned everyone else from eating them, so people paid a fortune
exercise: Why does honey not go bad? | It has very little water and is a bit acidic, so germs cannot grow | It is kept in gold jars | Bees put a lot of salt in it, which stops it from going off for years | It is always kept in a fridge
exercise: Who should never be given honey? | Babies under one year | Grandparents | Athletes | Children over five years old
exercise: In which country are guinea pigs a traditional food? | Peru | Japan | Ireland | Norway
exercise: How heavy can a prize-winning giant pumpkin be? | Over 1,000 kilograms | About 10 kilograms | About 100 grams | Over 100 tonnes
exercise: Where did coffee first come from? | Ethiopia | Italy | Brazil | Britain
test: «pizza's Italian, all of it, done» What is truer? | The tomatoes came from the Americas and the wheat from the Middle East | Every single part of it first grew in Italy, from the wheat to the tomatoes | Only the cheese came from abroad | Pizza is from America
test: «Irish stew with spuds, Irish since forever» What is right? | Potatoes came to Ireland from South America only after Columbus | Potatoes were first farmed in Ireland and taken from there to the rest of the world | Ireland grew potatoes 5,000 years ago | Potatoes came from China
test: «why's it called orange, cos it's orange obviously» What happened in English? | The colour was named after the fruit | The fruit was named after the colour | Both were named after a city | Neither has anything to do with the other
test: What would happen to vanilla farms outside Mexico if nobody pollinated the flowers by hand? | Very few pods would form | They would make twice as many pods | The flowers would turn into oranges | Nothing, vanilla needs no flowers
test: «snails?? frog legs?? gross, French people are weird» What is a fairer view? | Food is cultural, and others may find our food odd too | French food is just wrong | Only one country in the world eats properly, and that is ours | Snails cannot be eaten
test: «found 3,000-year-old honey in a tomb, that must be rotten» What did archaeologists find? | Honey can stay good for thousands of years | Old honey always turns to poison | It had become wine | It was all water
test: Count them: a pizza has 8 slices, each with 3 olives — no wait, 2 olives. How many olives? | 16 | 24 | 10 | 8
test: In a cartoon pirates fight over a chest of gold coins. What did real sailors also fight over long ago? | Spices such as nutmeg and pepper | Potatoes and carrots | Tins of baked beans | Bananas from Iceland's farms
test: «kiwi fruit, from New Zealand, named after the bird, so it's Kiwi from day one» What is right? | It came from China and was renamed in New Zealand | It was born in New Zealand | It is a kind of bird egg | It came from Brazil
test: Why could Inca soldiers carry chuño on long marches? | Freeze-dried potatoes are light and last for years | It weighs more than gold | It grows on the way | It must be eaten the same day it is made, so it was always fresh
test: «sushi = raw fish, any raw fish from the shop is fine» What is right? | Raw fish must be very fresh and handled carefully, sometimes frozen first | Yes, any raw fish from any shop is safe to eat, however long it has been there | Sushi never has fish in it | Raw fish is always poisonous
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Columbian exchange? | The transfer of crops, livestock and diseases between the Americas and the rest of the world following 1492 | A commercial agreement between Spain and Portugal | The exchange of currency in the ports of Genoa | The voyage of spices from Europe to Asia
exam: Identify the person credited with the discovery of an efficient method of pollinating vanilla by hand. | Edmond Albius, a twelve-year-old enslaved boy upon the island of Réunion | Christopher Columbus, upon his first voyage | An Aztec emperor of Mexico | A French professor of botany in Paris
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for the long preservation of honey. | Its low water content and slight acidity, which prevent the growth of microorganisms | The addition of salt by the bees | Its storage invariably in sealed vessels of gold | The presence of alcohol in substantial quantities, produced by the fermentation within the hive
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the kiwi fruit. | It originated in China and was subsequently renamed by growers in New Zealand | It originated in New Zealand and was named after the native bird by the earliest settlers | It originated in South America | It was first cultivated in Spain
